                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Near zero</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Extending the season on a budget</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you swim most days from May to September and live somewhere the nights stay above 50°F, a heat pump almost always wins. If you heat a pool for a few weekends, want the spa hot in 30 minutes, or live where spring air is cold, gas earns its higher fuel bill. If you mainly want the pool a few degrees warmer and the electric bill to stay put, start with solar and a cover.
          </p>
        </div>

