        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Size a Pool Heater
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heater sizing starts with the pool's surface area, because most heat leaves through the surface. The Department of Energy's sizing guidance for gas pool heaters uses a simple formula: multiply the pool's surface area in square feet by the temperature rise you want over the average air temperature of the coldest month you swim, then multiply by 12. A 16 by 32 foot pool (512 square feet) that you want 20°F above the air needs about 512 x 20 x 12, or roughly 123,000 BTU per hour. That figure assumes average wind and humidity; wind and an uncovered pool push it up.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate how long a heater takes to warm a pool, use the water's heat capacity: one BTU raises one pound of water by 1°F, and a gallon of water weighs about 8.34 lb. A 20,000-gallon pool holds about 166,800 lb of water, so raising it 10°F needs about 1.67 million BTU before losses. A 250,000 BTU gas heater at 84% efficiency delivers about 210,000 BTU per hour, so roughly eight hours in ideal conditions. A heat pump delivering 110,000 BTU per hour needs about 15 hours, and longer once the surface loses heat overnight. In practice, heat pumps are run continuously to hold a temperature rather than to raise it quickly.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size for the coldest month you swim, not the warmest.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A heater that keeps up in July can fall far behind in May. For heat pumps, size against the 50°F rating, not the 80°F headline.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For heat pumps, pick the size whose rated output at 50°F air still meets your requirement if you want to swim in spring and fall. That is why the HeatPro sell sheet's third row matters: the HP21124T drops from 110,000 BTU at 80°F to 75,000 BTU at 50°F. If your calculated need is 100,000 BTU in May, the 140,000 BTU HP21404T or HP31404T is the safer choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above-ground pools are smaller and need far less. A 21-foot round pool at about 10,000 gallons is the territory of a 50,000 to 60,000 BTU heat pump such as the FibroPool FH055, or several solar panels. Buying an in-ground-sized heater for an above-ground pool wastes money and can exceed the flow your small pump delivers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running Cost: The Real Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running cost is where heat pumps earn their price. To compare fairly, look at the cost of delivering 100,000 BTU of heat into the water. We will use 17 cents per kWh for electricity, $1.50 per therm for natural gas and $2.75 per gallon for propane; swap in your own rates.
          </p>

          <p>
            A heat pump at a COP of 5 delivers five units of heat for every unit of electricity. 100,000 BTU divided by 3,412 BTU per kWh is about 29.3 kWh of heat, and at COP 5 that takes about 5.9 kWh of electricity, or about $1.00. At 50°F air and COP 4, the same heat takes about 7.3 kWh, roughly $1.25.
          </p>

          <p>
            A natural gas heater at 84% efficiency needs about 119,000 BTU of gas to deliver 100,000 BTU, which is about 1.19 therms, or roughly $1.79. Propane holds about 91,500 BTU per gallon, so the same heat takes about 1.3 gallons, roughly $3.58.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Cost per 100,000 BTU Delivered (Our Assumptions)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.00</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Heat pump, COP 5</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.25</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Heat pump, COP 4 (50°F air)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$1.79</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Natural gas, 84%</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.58</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Propane, 84%</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Scale that to a real job. Raising a 20,000-gallon pool by 10°F needs about 1.67 million BTU before losses: roughly $17 with a heat pump, $30 with natural gas and $60 with propane. Holding temperature across a season costs far more, because the pool loses heat every night, which is why a cover matters more than any heater choice. The Department of Energy notes that pool covers can cut heating costs substantially, because evaporation is the largest source of heat loss.
          </p>

          <p>
            These numbers explain the usual recommendation. If you heat all season, a heat pump repays its higher purchase price quickly. If you heat only for occasional weekends, the gas heater's lower purchase price and speed may win despite the fuel cost. Solar has no fuel cost at all, which is why it pairs so well with either.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Installation: Electrical, Gas and Plumbing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heat pumps need a dedicated 240V circuit sized to the nameplate. Hayward lists a 42-amp minimum circuit and a 60-amp maximum breaker for the HP21124T, which usually means a new run from your panel, a disconnect within sight of the unit and wire sized for the distance. That work belongs to a licensed electrician and normally needs a permit. If your panel is already near capacity, the heat pump may be the trigger for a panel upgrade, which changes the cost comparison.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gas heaters need far less electricity; Hayward lists 5.5 circuit amps for the H-Series. The hard part is gas supply. A 250,000 BTU heater can need a larger gas line than a typical house run, and a 400,000 BTU heater often needs a larger meter, which involves the gas utility. A plumber or gas fitter should size the line for the full load of every gas appliance in the house running at once. Gas heaters also need clearances from walls, windows and vents; Hayward lists 6 inches at the rear and 12 inches at the sides for the H-Series, and local code may require more.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plumbing goes after the filter and before any chlorinator, so concentrated chlorine never passes through the heat exchanger. Most heaters use 2-inch connections and list a flow range; the HeatPro line lists 30 to 75 GPM. Too little flow trips the pressure switch; too much can require a bypass valve. A check valve between the heater and a salt cell or chlorinator protects the exchanger when the pump stops.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set heat pumps on a level pad with clear airflow around them, because they move a lot of air and need room to breathe. Keep them away from bedroom windows; the fan and compressor are about as loud as a central air conditioner. Gas heaters need the same level pad and enough distance from anything combustible. On either type, a surge protector at the panel, such as those in our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector guide</a>, protects the control board.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="above-ground" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Heating an Above-Ground Pool
          </h2>

          <p>
            Above-ground pools hold less water and have less surface area, so a modest heater goes a long way. A 55,000 BTU heat pump like the FibroPool FH055 is the usual match for pools up to about 10,000 gallons, and solar panels are often enough on their own in sunny climates. Gas heaters work, but most are sized for in-ground pools and burn fuel faster than an above-ground pool needs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check your pump before buying any heater. Above-ground pumps often move 40 to 60 GPM, which sits within the range most small heat pumps want, but some in-ground heaters list minimum flows above what a small pump can deliver. The heater's flow range should overlap your pump's output at your system's resistance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Above-ground walls also lose heat through the sides, which an in-ground pool does not, so a solar cover matters even more. A cover plus one or two solar panels can add several degrees for very little money; add a small heat pump if you want reliable heat on cloudy weeks or into autumn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about where the heater sits. Above-ground pools often sit on a deck or patio, and a heat pump needs a level, solid base and airflow. Keep it away from the pool wall so it cannot be used as a step into the pool, which is a safety issue as well as a durability one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Maintenance and Lifespan
          </h2>

          <p>
            Water chemistry is the biggest factor in heater life. Low pH and high chlorine corrode heat exchangers, especially copper ones, so keep pH and chlorine within the ranges your test kit recommends and never feed chlorine tablets upstream of the heater. Cupro-nickel and titanium exchangers tolerate salt and chemicals better, which is why salt-pool owners should choose them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heat pumps need clear coils and good airflow. Hose the evaporator coil off gently each season, keep plants and debris back, and make sure the condensate drains away from the pad. A heat pump that is starved of air works harder and heats less. Gas heaters need a yearly look at the burner tray, the venting and the pressure switch, and many owners have a technician service them before the season starts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winterizing matters wherever water freezes. Drain the heater following the manual before the first freeze; trapped water expands and cracks heat exchangers and headers. Solar panels need draining too, and some owners blow the lines out with a shop vac. A heater that survives one hard freeze undrained is lucky, not durable.
          </p>

          <p>
            With good chemistry and seasonal care, heat pumps and gas heaters commonly last many years, but the heat exchanger is usually the part that decides the end. Keep the warranty paperwork and buy through a channel that honors the full term; the difference between dealer and online warranties is one of the quieter costs in this category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing a Heat Pump to the 80°F Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heat pump output falls as the air cools. The HeatPro HP21124T drops from 110,000 BTU at 80°F to 75,000 BTU at 50°F. If you swim in spring or fall, size to the lower number, or the heater will run all day and still fall short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Pool Cover</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Evaporation is the biggest source of heat loss, so an uncovered pool throws away much of what any heater adds. A cover often saves more than the difference between heater models and should be part of the budget from day one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Gas Supply</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 250,000 or 400,000 BTU heater can starve other appliances or run poorly if the gas line or meter is too small. Have a gas fitter size the line for every gas appliance running at once before you buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the Chlorinator Before the Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Concentrated chlorine from a tablet feeder or salt cell corrodes heat exchangers. Plumb the heater after the filter and before the chlorinator, with a check valve, as the manuals specify.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to Drain for Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Water trapped in a heat exchanger or solar panel freezes, expands and cracks it. Drain every heater and panel before the first freeze, following the manual, even if you plan to open the pool early.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

