          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best type of pool heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most in-ground pools used all season in warm or mild climates, a heat pump is the best value because it delivers several units of heat per unit of electricity. Gas is better for spas, occasional use and cold climates, and solar is the cheapest way to add a few degrees. Many owners combine solar or a heat pump with gas.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size pool heater do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Department of Energy guidance for gas heaters multiplies pool surface area by the temperature rise you want over the coldest month's average air temperature, then by 12. A 512 square foot pool needing a 20°F rise works out near 123,000 BTU per hour. For heat pumps, check the rating at 50°F air if you swim in spring or fall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to run a pool heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Using 17 cents per kWh, $1.50 per therm and $2.75 per gallon of propane, delivering 100,000 BTU costs about $1.00 with a heat pump at COP 5, $1.79 with natural gas and $3.58 with propane. Season costs depend mostly on how much heat the pool loses, which a cover reduces sharply.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do pool heat pumps work in cold weather?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They work less well. Most standard models stop heating around 50°F air, and output drops as the air cools. Hayward lists its standard HeatPro models down to 50°F and its low-ambient models down to 40°F. Below that, a gas heater is the reliable option.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a pool heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The plumbing is within reach of a handy owner, but the electrical and gas work generally is not. Heat pumps need a dedicated 240V circuit sized to the nameplate, and gas heaters need a properly sized gas line and venting. Both normally require permits and licensed trades.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to heat a pool?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Raising a 20,000-gallon pool 10°F needs about 1.67 million BTU before losses. A 250,000 BTU gas heater can do it in roughly eight hours in ideal conditions; a 110,000 BTU heat pump takes closer to a day or more. Heat pumps are usually run continuously to hold temperature.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
